  <h2>More than designing buildings</h2>
  <p>Ask most people what an architect does and they will say "designs buildings". True, but that is a fraction of the job. An architect guides a project through its whole life - understanding what a client needs, testing ideas, navigating planning and building regulations, producing the technical information a builder works from, and helping keep the project on track on site. Much of the week is spent communicating: drawing, yes, but also writing, emailing, meeting and coordinating a cast of engineers, consultants, contractors and clients. Design is the heart of it, but design is only powerful when it survives contact with budgets, rules and reality.</p>

  <h2>The RIBA work stages</h2>
  <p>UK projects are usually organised around the RIBA Plan of Work, which breaks a project into stages from first idea to life in use. You do not need to memorise these now, but recognising them helps the whole profession make sense.</p>

  <table class="data"><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
    <tr><td>0-1 Brief</td><td>Define what the client needs and gather site information</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2 Concept</td><td>Develop the initial design idea and overall approach</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3 Spatial coordination</td><td>Refine the design and coordinate with engineers</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4 Technical design</td><td>Produce detailed drawings and specifications to build from</td></tr>
    <tr><td>5 Construction</td><td>Support the build, answer queries and inspect progress on site</td></tr>
    <tr><td>6-7 Handover &amp; use</td><td>Complete, hand over and review the building in use</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>The day-to-day</h2>
  <p>A real day rarely looks like the romantic image. You might spend the morning refining a plan in CAD or a BIM model, take a call with a structural engineer about a beam that clashes with a duct, write a specification clause, and sketch three options for a stair over lunch. Afternoons often bring a client meeting or a site visit, where you check that what is being built matches the drawings and troubleshoot the inevitable surprises. There is admin - programmes, fee letters, emails - because someone has to keep the machine moving. The mix of creative and practical is exactly what many architects love, but it is worth knowing the balance before you commit.</p>

  <div class="callout"><strong>Tip.</strong> If you get the chance, ask an architect to walk you through one real project from brief to completion. Seeing how a single building moved through the stages teaches you more than any abstract description of the job.</div>

  <h2>Common misconceptions</h2>
  <p>A few myths are worth clearing up early. Architects do not personally build the building - contractors do; the architect designs it and helps oversee the work. It is not all glamorous, headline-grabbing design; a great deal of the value lies in careful technical work and problem-solving that no one ever notices. You are rarely a lone genius - buildings are team sports involving engineers, planners, cost consultants and clients, and coordinating that team is a core skill. And it is not instantly lucrative; the training is long and early salaries are modest, so it is a career you choose for the work itself. None of this makes it less rewarding - but a clear-eyed picture beats a disappointed one.</p>

  <h2>Specialisms</h2>
  <p>"Architect" is not a single job. As you progress you can lean into whatever grips you. Some architects specialise by sector - housing, healthcare, education, conservation of historic buildings, or large-scale masterplanning. Others specialise by skill: sustainability and low-carbon design, technical detailing, computational design, or the increasingly important world of BIM management. Some move toward the front end and become brilliant at winning work and running client relationships; others go deep into research or teaching. You do not need to choose now. Early years are for broad exposure, and most architects only find their niche after trying several kinds of project.</p>

  <h2>Is it the job you imagined?</h2>
  <p>For many students, learning what the role really involves makes it more appealing, not less - the variety, the mix of art and problem-solving, and the fact that your work ends up as a real place people use. For others it is a useful reality check. Either way, understanding the actual work now helps you shape a career, or a study path like <a href="/career/how-to-become-an-architect/">becoming an architect</a>, around what you genuinely enjoy.</p>

  <div class="callout warn"><strong>Routes vary.</strong> Qualification paths, titles and figures differ by country and change over time - always check the current requirements of your national registration body (e.g. ARB/RIBA in the UK, NCARB in the US).</div>

